<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint Nino</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">4th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Saint Nina, Nino of Cappadocia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saint Nino was a woman, traditionally from Cappadocia, who brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> in the early 4th century. According to tradition her healing of Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanaya">Nana</a> and her prayers led King Mirian III to adopt Christianity. Her grapevine cross became a symbol of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgian-orthodox-church">Georgian Orthodox Church</a>, and she is buried at Bodbe monastery in Kakheti.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-nino.jpg" alt="Saint Nino" width="320" height="379" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ackerman200 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St._Nino_Icon1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The oldest surviving account of Nino is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> one by Rufinus in his Historia ecclesiastica, composed around 403, which rests on what a Georgian prince named Bacurius told him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>. Later writers include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, Sozomen and Theodoret of Cyrrhus, and the 10th-century Georgian Conversion of Kartli largely repeats them. Her ethnic background is not clearly known. According to tradition she was born in Cappadocia into a Greek-speaking Roman family, and her father was the commander Zabulon and her mother Susanna. Georgian church tradition also makes her a relative of Saint George.</p><p>At twelve she went with her parents to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, where her parents entered religious life and she was entrusted to a devout woman, Sarah Miafore. According to tradition she later fled the persecution of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> with a group of women who were martyred in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, and only she escaped. In a vision the Virgin Mary gave her a grapevine cross.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Nino crossed the Javakheti mountains into the Kingdom of Kartli and stayed a month in Urbnisi among the Jewish community to learn local customs. She learned Georgian and began to preach, gaining followers such as Salome of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujarma">Ujarma</a>. Her missionary work was accompanied by miraculous healings, among them that of Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanaya">Nana</a>, who converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. King Mirian III at first persecuted the new faith. According to legend he was struck blind while hunting and, praying to the God of Nino, found light and his way back to his palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mtskheta">Mtskheta</a>. He was baptised as the first Christian king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> and, in about 326, made Christianity the state religion.</p><p>Mirian then petitioned the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a> to send clergy for the baptism of the population. Under Nino's guidance crosses were erected in the mountains where idols had stood, and churches were built at Svetitskhoveli and Nekresi among other places.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In her final years Nino lived at Bodbe in Kakheti, where she died, and Mirian soon began a monastery there. Her tomb is still venerated at the Bodbe Monastery. She is one of the most venerated saints of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgian-orthodox-church">Georgian Orthodox Church</a>, her main commemoration is on 14 January, and her grapevine cross is a symbol of Georgian Christianity. Nino and its variants remain the most popular name for girls and women in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saint Nino” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its conversion to Christianity under King Mirian III, traditionally through the preaching of St <b>Nino</b>, a woman from Cappadocia, is usually dated to the 320s or 330s, which makes Iberia one of the first states, after Armenia, to adopt Christianity as its official religion; the church historian Rufinus…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-iberia">Kingdom of Iberia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cappadocia, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in Bodbe Monastery, in what is now <a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.67,34.84&amp;year=364" aria-label="Cappadocia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5415 776 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Saint Nino"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5865 1046h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5865 1046h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.67,34.84&amp;year=364">What was here in 364 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
